	======================================================
	Computation of the Euclidean minimum of a number field
	======================================================

### BASIC PRESENTATION ###
Given a number field K (defined by an irreducible polynomial P with
integral coefficients), the program tries to compute 
   M(K) = sup m_K(x) for any element x of K, where
   m_K(x)  = min(N(x-X)) for X in the ring of integers of K,
it provides the points x for which M(K)= m_K(x) if the algorithm is 
successful.

### INSTALLATION ###
See INSTALL file.

### EXECUTION ###
Either type ./euclid and then a polynomial (as requested) or type
./euclid "x^2-61" or ./euclid [1,0,-61] (you can replace x^2-61
by any polynomial that you are interested in).

### MORE DETAILS ###
With the default parameters, few details are provided, you can have
more information by changing the configuration file euclid.cfg.

### LIMITATIONS AND PROBLEMS###
1. Precision is provided "by hand" in euclid.cfg and some care is
   required, especially when the degree and/or the discriminant grows.
2. There is no guarantee that a convenient graph will be obtained at any
   step, experience shows however that tweaking with the parameters makes
   the algorithm successful in most "reasonable" cases.
3. The algorithm does not work very well (or at all) when the units of
   the number field are "bad", that is to say the absolute value of their
   embeddings is too big or too small (e.g. the algorithm does not work
   presently for x^4-61).
4. The procedures' names are mostly french and will probably be quite
   difficult to understand for non-french speakers.

### REFERENCE ###
The procedures are the application of the article available at
<http://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/hal-00632997/>

### CHAPEL PORT ###
An arbitrary-degree Chapel reimplementation of this algorithm (floating
point kernel, Pari used only for one-time field setup and optional exact
certification) lives alongside this C tool. See CHAPEL.md for details.
